{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Therefore. And its glory.</b> The sense is: and a people like its glory, according to the rule of other [particles] that draw after them." "Likewise <b>parched with thirst</b> [is understood as] when [it is] parched, and the meaning of צחה is close to צמא, and likewise והשביע בצחצחות (Isaiah 58:11), and its derivation is the light that is זך and צח."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The comment addresses the syntactic force of the ל־ in לָכֵן גָּלָה עַמִּי 'therefore my people went into exile'; Ibn Ezra reads עַמִּי וּכְבוֹדוֹ together, treating the vav as connective. The phrase 'מלות מושכות אחרות' ('other words that draw after them') refers to particles that attract or govern a following element; the referent is not further specified.", 1 "The lemma צחה צמא (Isaiah 5:13) is a crux; Ibn Ezra reads it as two related terms. 'מתי צחה' is elliptical — literally 'when parched' — possibly implying a conditional or temporal gloss on the collocation. The final clause derives צחצחות from a root meaning clear/bright light, leaving זך and צח untranslated as Hebrew adjectives he cites as such."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:52:03.840682370Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}